{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling on 65fb24b3, the first BR-5 scale run on main: scale the deadlines on the GitHub runner only. Nothing else changes.\n\n1. Run 42570561287 stays failed as recorded: five cases passed, and the 30,000-entry case reached its 600 s deadline. No rerun stands in for it. The checker's table (d26cff57) shows every case 2.5 to 3.5 times slower on ubuntu-24.04 than in the local gate, with the four owners byte-equal, so I read it as runner speed, not a regression.\n2. Each scale case multiplies its explicit deadline by a runner factor read from one environment variable. Only the main scale workflow sets it, to 3. The local gate and the review gate leave it unset, so their deadlines stay exactly as they are. No entry count, bound or assertion changes. The deadline guards against a hang and makes no performance claim, so the factor keeps every acceptance witness.\n3. Raise the workflow's timeout-minutes so that the job can finish at factor 3, to 90 minutes.\n4. Your CI-only capture stands. Send the full runner output to a retained workflow artifact, and print a compact summary with each case's result and duration.\n5. Witness: the next main run either passes all six cases or names the case that failed and how long it ran.\n6. Not chosen: a larger GitHub runner. The repository is public, so standard runners cost nothing, and a larger runner is a paid plan feature. That is Hugh's decision. If he provides one, the factor returns to 1.\n\nFile this as one small head under your own request resting on this ruling. It goes next after BR-11's review, ahead of R13 item 4, because a main run that fails on every push catches nothing."}